# Keep README & wiki up to date

After any change that affects how operators or developers use Guartrix, **check and update docs in the same task** before finishing.

## When to update

Update docs if you change any of:

- Install / start / ports / env vars / systemd
- Auth, registration, quotas, mail, subusers
- Nodes, remote install, SFTP, daemon behaviour
- Security controls, public URLs, architecture, scaling assumptions
- User-facing UI flows that docs describe (e.g. Add node wizard)

Skip docs-only noise for pure refactors with **no** behaviour or ops change.

## What to touch

1. **[README.md](README.md)** — short hub: features, quick start, links. Keep it accurate; put detail in the wiki.
2. **[docs/wiki/](docs/wiki/)** — update the relevant page(s). Add a new page + link from `docs/wiki/README.md` if a topic is new.
3. If `.env.example` changes, sync **[docs/wiki/env-reference.md](docs/wiki/env-reference.md)** **and the live [`.env`](.env)** — see [keep-env-synced](keep-env-synced.mdc).

## How

- Prefer editing existing sections over duplicating long guides in the README.
- Match reality (commands, paths, defaults like `DEFAULT_MAX_SERVERS=0`, email verify, SSH modal).
- Do not leave “TODO” stubs in shipped docs.

## Example

```text
Changed: invite no longer returns temporaryPassword
→ Update docs/wiki/accounts-and-quotas.md (and security.md if listed)
→ Skim README “Accounts & quotas” blurb for stale wording
```
